H.264 host instruction regression tests
======================================

host.py extracts the production H.264 builders verbatim and compiles them
against a userspace shim. It compares complete header and slice instruction
programs with a supplied baseline. Synthetic DMA addresses make the output
reproducible. No decoder device or firmware is needed.

On a Linux build host, with an older driver directory in BASELINE:

  python3 tests/host.py --baseline BASELINE --out OUT
  python3 tests/queue.py --baseline BASELINE --out OUT

The comparison spans dense DPBs with zero through sixteen entries, I/P/B
slices, CAVLC/CABAC, explicit/implicit weighting, first/continuation slices,
and both address encodings. Dense programs must remain byte-identical.
Sparse programs may change only the DPB slot operand of reference-list
instructions. All 65536 DPB occupancy maps are checked independently.
The tests also exercise slice-type, slice-group and HEVC bit-depth rejection.
OUT/before.json and OUT/after.json retain the instruction programs for review.

These tests verify control translation and compatibility, not decoded pixels.
A hardware conformance rerun is still needed to demonstrate pixel correctness.

queue.py compiles the production queue initializer and format-feature update.
It reproduces FFmpeg capability probing before S_FMT and verifies that a
multislice picture reaches hardware once, after its last slice. It also checks
that changing formats removes stale per-format queue features.
